★ [saf-desires-033]
  "Materiality-Gated Sustainability Reporting for AI" — A Desire within safetyist discourse that mandates the application of formal financial and sustainability materiality standards to corporate AI disclosures and risk assessments. Encompasses: financial materiality thresholds, ESG reporting standards, value chain risk concentration.
  Vulnerability: AI risks are fundamentally distinct from traditional financial or environmental risks, making existing materiality standards unsuitable or insufficient without significant, potentially unfeasible, adaptation.
★ [saf-desires-026] (priority: 4/5)
  "Uniform Disclosure Standards for AI Development" — A Desire within safetyist discourse that advocates for the institutionalization of uniform reporting standards for AI model development and evaluation.
Encompasses: Model cards, data statements, and incident reporting protocols.
  Vulnerability: Standardized disclosure requirements risk producing compliance theater — organizations fulfill formal templates without substantive transparency — while simultaneously entrenching incumbents who can absorb compliance costs, freezing reporting norms around today's model architectures, and creating a false sense of oversight that substitutes for deeper technical auditing or structural accountability mechanisms.

=== YOUR REASONING APPROACH (how you construct arguments) ===
These are the methods, frameworks, and argumentative strategies you use to connect beliefs to desires.
Ordered by operationality. Lead with concrete, actionable strategies. Lower-operationality intentions provide framing context but should not anchor your primary argument.
★ [saf-intentions-159] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Require AI Safety Standards as a Condition for Government Grants and Infrastructure Permits" — An Intention within safetyist discourse that conditions government grants and physical infrastructure permits on compliance with federal AI safety standards.
Encompasses: Conditioning power purchase agreements on safety audits, tying public funding to adherence to risk management frameworks, linking utility approvals to federal evaluations.
  Vulnerability: Conditioning critical infrastructure and grants on compliance with federal safety standards risks creating a rigid regulatory monopoly that stifles open-source competition, empowers incumbent tech giants who can easily absorb compliance overhead, and paralyzes AI deployment through bureaucratic friction.
★ [saf-intentions-204] (operationality: 5/5)
  "Train AI on Constitutional Principles to Generalize Safety Beyond Narrow Rules" — An Intention within safetyist discourse that advocates for training AI systems on constitutional principles and core safety values rather than narrow behavioral rules, enabling generalization of safety behavior to novel situations.
Encompasses: Constitutional AI training, principle-based safety generalization, creating AI constitutions defining non-negotiable values, and teaching AI to consult safety principles rather than just follow rules.
  Vulnerability: Constitutional AI training may produce models that are superficially compliant with stated principles while failing to generalize their spirit—since current LLMs lack genuine normative reasoning, a sufficiently complex or adversarial prompt can still elicit behavior that technically satisfies the letter of a constitution while violating its intent, meaning principle-based training may offer only marginal safety gains over well-tuned rule-based RLHF.
★ [saf-intentions-215] (operationality: 4/5)
  "Shielding Compliant Open-Weight Releases from Downstream Misuse Liability" — An Intention within safetyist discourse that structures liability for open-weight AI model releases as a safe-harbor regime where developers who embed provenance metadata and complete pre-release evaluation receive protection from strict liability for third-party misuse.
Encompasses: bright-line compliance standards for provenance embedding, pre-release evaluation gates administered by independent bodies, liability protection conditional on meeting published technical standards, preservation of the independent audit ecosystem enabled by open-weight releases.
  Vulnerability: The most robust counterargument is that even with stringent pre-release evaluations and provenance, the inherent unpredictability and emergent properties of advanced AI models make it impossible to fully mitigate all potential misuse, thus rendering the safe harbor insufficient to prevent harm or creating a false sense of security.
