  "statement": "Safetyist argues that statutory audit access converts compliance burdens into public utilities that prevent corporate dominance. The steelman version of this position holds that when a neutral state agency holds the keys to pre-market testing, private labs lose the power to mark their own homework, thereby protecting the public from unverified commercial releases. That claim assumes a state agency can evaluate closed systems without relying on the very corporate infrastructure it aims to oversee.\n\nIn actual deployment, independent statutory oversight relies entirely on proprietary tooling, compute clusters, and proprietary training logs owned by a handful of commercial labs. When a compliance regime mandates complex evaluations of frontier code, the testing body must contract with private vendors or hire engineers trained exclusively inside those same dominant firms. This structural dependency turns the state auditor into a downstream client of the enterprise it regulates, transferring public authority directly into private ledgers. The legal overhead and testing fees then form an insuperable barrier that small teams and academic developers cannot clear, leaving the compliance market partitioned among incumbents.\n\nWhen compliance mechanisms require resources only large enterprises possess, the regulatory apparatus creates the exact consolidation it claims to prevent. The compliance officers, certified testing consultancies, and corporate legal departments profit from the moat, while open-source developers face criminal exposure or market exclusion. No statutory declaration of independence can alter the material fact that checking the code requires the hardware of the firm that wrote it. Who answers when the independent testing mandate permanently locks out every competitor except the original duopoly?",
